Exercise 2.15 takes the one-token system \(\mathcal{O}=\{\{0\},\emptyset\}\), regards \(|\mathcal{O}|\) as two elements \(\bot\sqsubseteq\top\), and asks for a bijection between the open subsets \(U\subseteq|D|\) and the approximable maps \(f\colon D\to\mathcal{O}\) given by \(U=\{x\in|D|\mid f(x)=\top\}\), together with the open subsets of \(|\mathcal{O}|\), of \(|\mathbb{T}|\), and of \(|\mathbb{B}|\). `O` is that system on a one-point token set: `memO` says the neighbourhoods are the universe and the empty set, `O_master` makes the universe the master, and `O_inter_mem` closes them under intersection. `topElt` is the principal filter of the empty neighbourhood, `botElt` is the bottom element, `mem_empty_iff_eq_top` says an element contains \(\emptyset\) exactly when it is top, `element_eq_bot_or_top` says every element is one of these two, and `botElt_le_topElt` together with `botElt_ne_topElt` give the strict order \(\bot\sqsubseteq\top\). `mapToOpen` sends an approximable map to the set of elements it carries to top, and `isOpen_mapToOpen` shows that set is open because it is the preimage of the basic open of \(\emptyset\) under the continuous element map. `openToMap` sends an open \(U\) to the relation on which emptiness of the output neighbourhood implies that the principal filter of the input neighbourhood lies in \(U\); the master is related because the master of \(\mathcal{O}\) is nonempty, and the intersection and monotonicity laws split on whether the output is the universe or empty. `mem_mapToOpen_openToMap` and `openToMap_mapToOpen` are the two round trips, `openSet_equiv_map` is the bijection, and `example_215` is the conjunction of those round trips. `basicOpen_empty_eq_singleton_top` gives \([\emptyset]=\{\top\}\) and `not_isOpen_singleton_botElt` shows that \(\{\bot\}\) is not open, which is the start of a description of the opens of \(|\mathcal{O}|\), but the opens are not listed, and \(|\mathbb{T}|\) and \(|\mathbb{B}|\) do not appear. **Theorem:** partial — the bijection \(U=\{x\mid f(x)=\top\}\) is `openSet_equiv_map`, while the open subsets of \(|\mathcal{O}|\), \(|\mathbb{T}|\), and \(|\mathbb{B}|\) are not identified. **Proof:** partial — the emptiness relation, the preimage of \([\emptyset]\), and the two round trips prove the correspondence; the three classification questions have no argument.
